From: rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] inputmethod: a user-forced keyboard that survives non-text-input surfaces

The on-screen keyboard is only ever raised for a surface that speaks the
Wayland text-input protocol. XWayland clients cannot speak it, so Java/X11
applications (Burp Suite, and every other XWayland window) get a text field, a
cursor and no keyboard. The DBus escape hatch forceActivate() exists but does
not persist: a later refreshActive() -- fired by any text-input
enabled-changed signal, or by show()'s no-panel branch -- re-imposes the
text-input gate and calls setActive(false), and setTrackedWindow() clears
m_shouldShowPanel on the next focus change, so the forced keyboard is torn down
within a moment of appearing.

This adds a single piece of state, m_forcedActive, set by forceActivate() and
cleared when the user dismisses the keyboard (hide()). While it is set:

  - refreshActive() forces active = true instead of consulting text-input, so
    text-input signals cannot deactivate the keyboard;
  - shouldShowOnActive() returns true (as KWIN_IM_SHOW_ALWAYS already does), so
    the panel is shown even though the last input on an XWayland window was a
    mouse or keyboard rather than touch;
  - setTrackedWindow() does not reset m_shouldShowPanel and re-shows the panel,
    so a focus change keeps the keyboard up instead of dropping it.

Keystrokes reach XWayland because plasma-keyboard emits keycodes through
zwp_virtual_keyboard_v1, which KWin routes to the seat's keyboard focus (the
focused window, XWayland included) rather than through a text-input channel. An
input method that instead *commits strings* through text-input would need KWin
to synthesise key events for surfaces with no text-input; that is a larger
change and is deliberately out of scope here -- see this directory's README for
how to tell the two apart on the device before trusting this patch.

Against KWin Plasma/6.2 (src/inputmethod.cpp, src/inputmethod.h). The hunks are
anchored on stable API (forceActivate, refreshActive, shouldShowOnActive,
setTrackedWindow) and apply with fuzz on neighbouring 6.x releases.
---
diff --git a/src/inputmethod.cpp b/src/inputmethod.cpp
index ec13035..2adf711 100644
--- a/src/inputmethod.cpp
+++ b/src/inputmethod.cpp
@@ -178,6 +178,9 @@ void InputMethod::show()
 
 void InputMethod::hide()
 {
+    // A user dismissing the keyboard clears the forced-on state; the next
+    // text-input-driven surface decides on its own whether to raise it again.
+    m_forcedActive = false;
     m_shouldShowPanel = false;
     if (m_panel) {
         m_panel->hide();
@@ -188,7 +191,11 @@ void InputMethod::hide()
 bool InputMethod::shouldShowOnActive() const
 {
     static bool alwaysShowIm = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("KWIN_IM_SHOW_ALWAYS") != 0;
-    return alwaysShowIm || input()->touch() == input()->lastInputHandler()
+    // A user-forced keyboard (forceActivate over an XWayland / non-text-input
+    // surface) is driven by mouse or keyboard, so neither touch nor tablet is
+    // the last input handler; show it anyway, the same escape hatch that
+    // KWIN_IM_SHOW_ALWAYS provides.
+    return m_forcedActive || alwaysShowIm || input()->touch() == input()->lastInputHandler()
         || input()->tablet() == input()->lastInputHandler();
 }
 
@@ -199,6 +206,15 @@ void InputMethod::refreshActive()
     auto t2 = seat->textInputV2();
     auto t3 = seat->textInputV3();
 
+    // If the user forced the keyboard on (e.g. over an XWayland window that
+    // cannot speak text-input), keep it active regardless of what the focused
+    // surface supports. Without this, any text-input enabled-changed signal
+    // would call setActive(false) and tear the forced keyboard back down.
+    if (m_forcedActive) {
+        setActive(true);
+        return;
+    }
+
     bool active = false;
     if (auto focusedSurface = seat->focusedTextInputSurface()) {
         auto client = focusedSurface->client();
@@ -292,11 +308,19 @@ void InputMethod::setTrackedWindow(Window *trackedWindow)
         disconnect(m_trackedWindow, &Window::frameGeometryChanged, this, &InputMethod::updateInputPanelState);
     }
     m_trackedWindow = trackedWindow;
-    m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    // A focus change normally hides the panel (m_shouldShowPanel = false). Keep
+    // a user-forced keyboard up across focus changes so it does not vanish the
+    // instant the pointer moves to another window.
+    if (!m_forcedActive) {
+        m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    }
     if (m_trackedWindow) {
         connect(m_trackedWindow, &Window::frameGeometryChanged, this, &InputMethod::updateInputPanelState, Qt::QueuedConnection);
     }
     updateInputPanelState();
+    if (m_forcedActive) {
+        show();
+    }
 }
 
 void InputMethod::handleFocusedSurfaceChanged()
@@ -1006,6 +1030,7 @@ bool InputMethod::activeClientSupportsTextInput() const
 
 void InputMethod::forceActivate()
 {
+    m_forcedActive = true;
     setActive(true);
     show();
 }
diff --git a/src/inputmethod.h b/src/inputmethod.h
index d626824..c6e91cf 100644
--- a/src/inputmethod.h
+++ b/src/inputmethod.h
@@ -147,6 +147,12 @@ private:
     bool m_hasPendingModifiers = false;
     bool m_activeClientSupportsTextInput = false;
     bool m_shouldShowPanel = false;
+    // Set by forceActivate() (the DBus "show keyboard" entry point), cleared
+    // when the user dismisses the keyboard via hide(). While set, the text-input
+    // gate in refreshActive() is bypassed, shouldShowOnActive() returns true and
+    // focus changes do not drop the panel -- which is what lets the keyboard be
+    // raised over an XWayland / Java window that cannot speak text-input.
+    bool m_forcedActive = false;
 };
 
 }
